// T4 belt-and-braces gate (I5): an ordinary `?*T` local releases cleanly,
// with NO destructor call.
//
// `?*int` and `error` share ONE lowering: TYPE_NULLABLE of a pointer lowers
// to `{ i1, ptr }` (field 0 the nil flag, field 1 the object), so the SAME
// shape test in codegen_arc_classify_heap_field accepts both and sets
// field = 1 for both. Telling them apart is the DTOR SELECTION's job, not
// the release arm's: codegen_arc_note_local gates goo_error_dtor behind
// type_is_error (plus, since M9, a redundant `field == 1` structural
// check), so the destructor attaches ONLY to a real error. A `?*int` has
// no `message` field, so running goo_error_dtor on one would goo_release
// whatever garbage bytes sit at that struct offset.
//
// This is not a theoretical worry. The nullable-pointer arm's own commit
// message names it as load-bearing, and a manual, throwaway fixture proved
// it during that change's review (task-2-report.md, "Mutant 3"): widening
// the dtor test past type_is_error made THIS SAME SHAPE crash under
// valgrind with "Conditional jump or move depends on uninitialised
// value(s)". This file promotes that fixture to a permanent probe, wired
// into the arc-release-probe Makefile gate, so a future widening of the
// dtor condition is caught by `make verify-core` and not just by a
// reviewer who happens to remember to check by hand.
//
// Called in a loop so a leak is measurable rather than theoretical.
package main

var count int

func work() {
	var p ?*int = new(int)
	if p != nil {
		count = count + 1
	}
}

func main() {
	for i := 0; i < 10000; i++ {
		work()
	}
	println(count)
}
